# tlo: jesien
# rozdzial: Chapter Two
# tytul: School
Summer ended the way every summer ends – without anyone noticing. One morning a ground frost nipped the grass, and the swallows that had darted over the yard since spring sat in a row along the ridge of the barn roof, as if holding council over something. The next day they were gone. The leaves of the elder above the den turned yellow and dropped at every gust, and from the fields drifted the smoke of bonfires in which the peasants burned the withered potato haulms.
That autumn Jaromir turned nine, and for the first time he was to go to school.
{ figurka_u_staruchy:
    His father was against it for a long time. “Letters won’t plough a field,” he muttered over supper, and there the talk would have ended, had it not been for Agnieszka. She kept at him for three evenings, until at last Dobrosław rose from the table, took his one good cap down from its nail and, without a word, put it on his son’s head. It was too big, but the boy wore it with pride.
- else:
    His father was prouder of it than of anything else. He had never learned to read, and though he did not say so aloud, the boy had seen how at every Mass in the little church his father ran a finger along the letters in his wife’s prayer book, pretending to understand. “You’ll read for the both of us,” he said, and nailed new soles to his son’s boots.
}
His mother sewed him a shoulder bag from linen cloth, and his father brought back from the fair a slate in a wooden frame and a slate pencil that squeaked so that it sent shivers down your spine. # pokaz: jaromir
The school was in Bukowiec, on the far side of Beech Ridge – an hour’s walk if you didn’t stop to rest. The four of them went together: Franek, who had one last year of schooling left, Antek, Hanka and Jaromir. # dalej
-> szkola_mgla

= szkola_mgla
# tlo: droga_bor
On the first day they set out before dawn. A mist lay over the whole village, so thick that the wayside shrine by the footbridge loomed out of it only when you were right beside it. Franek walked in front, humming something under his breath; Hanka kept close to Jaromir, and Antek, as always, lagged a little behind, because something was forever catching his eye – now a cobweb full of dew, now a hare that bolted from a field balk.
The path climbed uphill along the edge of the forest. Fields stretched away on the left; on the right rose a wall of spruces, black and wet with mist. When they were roughly halfway up the slope, Antek suddenly stopped.
“Can you hear that?”
A voice came from deep in the woods. Low, calm, monotonous, as if someone were standing among the trees, counting something.
“…four hundred and one… four hundred and two… four hundred and three…”
“Woodcutters,” said Franek, though somehow more quietly than usual. “They’re counting the trunks for felling.”
Only woodcutters don’t count at dawn, in a mist, in a place where neither axe nor saw can be heard. Whoever it was came no closer and moved no farther off, but simply kept on counting.
