= szkola_podania
# tlo: szkola_podania
The best thing about school was the last half-hour on Fridays. If nobody had got up to mischief all week, Mr Ignacy would take off his spectacles, wipe them with his handkerchief, sit on the edge of the desk and tell stories. He knew tales from all over the district – of treasure buried beneath wayside shrines, of a hanged man who appeared at the crossroads, of a girl who danced herself to death with a young man nobody knew. He wrote them all down in that thick notebook he kept in the drawer.
That Friday he spoke of the one who guards the forest.
“Old folk have different names for him,” he began, and the room grew so quiet that you could hear the logs crackling in the stove. “Some name him Borowy, others the Lord of the Forest, and the oldest folk not at all, for it is wiser not to call him by name. They say he lives in the deepest heart of the forest and guards it as a farmer guards his farmstead. Whoever goes in there without asking, him he leads astray: a man thinks he is walking straight, yet he goes round in circles until night falls.” # pokaz: cien
Someone at the back giggled. Jaromir didn’t. He looked at Antek, then at Hanka – both of them were pale.
“And how can you protect yourself from him?” asked Franek.
Mr Ignacy nodded, as if he had been waiting for that question.
“You must take off your clothes and put them on inside out. Then Borowy gets muddled, because he thinks one of his own is standing before him.”
The whole room roared with laughter. The teacher joined in with the children, pushed his spectacles back up and sent everyone home. But as Jaromir was leaving, last of all, he saw Mr Ignacy standing at the window and drawing the linen curtain, though dusk was still a long way off. # pokaz: okno # dalej
