= wstazka
# tlo: wstazka_wiatr
In October the winds came. They blew from the west, off the lake, and bent the spruces so that they creaked and groaned all night long. One Saturday, as they were coming back from Bukowiec, a gust was so strong that it tore the ribbon from Hanka’s plait – the one from her father, which she wore every day. The wind whirled it through the air, carried it over their heads and flung it in among the trees.
The ribbon caught on the branch of a young pine, perhaps twenty paces into the forest. It hung there, fluttering, red as a drop of blood against the black-green needles.
Hanka bit her lip. She wasn’t crying, but Jaromir saw how her hands trembled.
# pytanie: Will Jaromir go after the ribbon?
*   [Go after the ribbon.]
    ~ poszedl_po_wstazke = true
    # tlo: bor_starzec
    “I’ll be right back,” he said, and before anyone could stop him, he stepped in among the trees.
    Under the spruces it was darker than he had expected, and strangely still – the wind that out in the open tugged at their clothes barely stirred the tips of the branches here. He walked straight towards the red patch. Twenty paces. Thirty. The ribbon still hung ahead of him, just as far away as at the start. He looked back – the path could no longer be seen. Only trunks, all alike, and grey mist between them.
    That was when he saw him.
    Not far off, in the half-light, stood a tall old man – so tall that he would have had to stoop to come into their hut. He wore a sheepskin turned fleece-out, belted with a cord, and a hat of moss with a broad, drooping brim. No face could be seen beneath it – only two bright points of eyes and a thick, matted beard. He did not move, leaning on a knotty staff from which leaves were sprouting, and in his hand something red was fluttering. # pokaz: starzec
    Jaromir wanted to scream, but his voice stuck in his throat. He blinked.
    The old man was gone. The ribbon hung on a branch right in front of the boy, within arm’s reach, and behind his back, a few steps away, the path showed through the trees and he could hear Hanka calling his name. # pokaz: zniknal
    He came out of the forest clutching the ribbon in his fist. The girl threw her arms around his neck, then, embarrassed, sprang away and did not meet his eyes the whole way home. Only at the footbridge did they notice that the sun already stood low over the lake, though when he had gone in among the trees it had been barely past noon. # dalej
