# tlo: szkola_ksiega
For several days Jaromir carried what had happened around inside him. It chafed like a stone in his boot. At last, on Friday after lessons, when the others had already run out into the yard, he stayed behind in the schoolroom and went to stand by Mr Ignacy’s desk. It smelled here of damp sheepskins and of smoke from the tiled stove, which crackled quietly in the corner.
He told him everything – about the counting in the mist, about the spiral on the pine, {poszedl_po_wstazke:about the old man in the sheepskin turned fleece-out|about the ribbon that had come back to the fence by itself}. He expected the teacher to laugh, as the whole class had done that day. But Mr Ignacy listened in silence, and with every sentence his face grew graver.
{ ekwipunek ? kora:
    When Jaromir took the piece of bark out of his bag, Mr Ignacy studied the marks carved on it for a long time. Then he reached into his desk and compared them with a drawing in his notes. His hands were shaking. # pokaz: kora
}
Mr Ignacy took off his spectacles and slowly wiped them on the hem of his waistcoat – he always did that when something stirred him.
“Don’t tell anyone about this,” he said at last. “And certainly not the grown-ups. They won’t believe you, they’ll laugh at you, and you’ll learn that in such matters it’s better to hold your tongue. And that is not how it ought to be.”
He took the thick leather-bound notebook from the drawer and laid it on the desk. On the first page, in a careful, slanting hand, was written: “Tales from around Beech Ridge, set down by Ignacy Zawada.” Jaromir gazed at the inscription with curiosity, but with fear as well. # pokaz: ksiega
“For thirty years I’ve been writing down here what people say in the villages. Perhaps you’ll find something in this book about what you saw. Only open it by daylight. Such things are not read after dark, for he who reads, calls.”
