    At night, when everyone in the house was asleep, he would pull Mr Ignacy’s book out from under his straw mattress and read it by the glow of the embers in the stove, though he knew he was only allowed to reach for it by day.
*   [Wait until morning to read.]
    In the evenings the book lay under the straw mattress and Jaromir did not touch it, though it tempted him more than the warm stove. He read at dawn, by the window, before he set off for school, and on Sundays after church, while it was still light.
-   There were legends of Lady Midday, who in the fiercest heat sets riddles for the reapers, of Lady Midnight, who torments infants, of rusalki and strzygas. Among the closely written pages someone had drawn an old man in clothes turned inside out and a hat of moss. Beneath the drawing it said: “Whoever meets him, let him do just as he does. That one comes from the other side, where everything is the wrong way round.”
